WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.420
We all want to eat
the kinds of foods

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.340
that make us feel
better and live longer.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.400
But there’s so much conflicting
information out there,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.920
so many nutrition opinions.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.100
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.900
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.600
It’s my job to give you
the information you need

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.384
to make the healthiest
choices possible.

00:00:21.384 --> 00:00:26.741
Today we take a close look at the
pros and cons of plant-based eating.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.900
Vegetarian diets and lifestyles
have been shown to reduce the risk

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.300
of many chronic diseases,
which now accounts

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.420
for the major global burden
of disease,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.220
but the actual direct medical costs
had never been quantified before.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.260
Here's what they found.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.820
The same amount spent

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.160
on dental work, but
compared to meat-eaters

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.440
who similarly don't
smoke or drink,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.240
or compared to
the general population,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.560
vegetarians had significantly
lower inpatient, outpatient,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.460
and total medical care expenditures,
suggesting more plant-based eating

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.820
could be an effective strategy
to save on healthcare costs.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.980
Here's how it broke down.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.160
Significantly lower costs
for chronic lifestyle conditions

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.020
such as high blood pressure
and high cholesterol.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.340
This makes sense.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.640
Those eating plant-based diets
centered around whole plant foods

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.580
nailed the targets for
cholesterol, triglycerides,

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.600
and systolic and diastolic blood
pressure 93% of the time,

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.820
97% of the time, 88% of the time,
and 95% of the time, respectively.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.710
Nearly a 50% drop in medical costs
due to depression, too --

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.040
that's interesting -- as well as
lower costs across the board.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.700
Cerebrovascular disease
is another name for stroke.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.560
Wasn't there that study that showed
vegetarians had higher stroke risk?

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.240
True, but that was before

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.420
two subsequent studies found
a lower risk of stroke

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.640
with a vegetarian diet,
and not just by a little.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.320
For ischemic stroke, the most
common clotting type of stroke,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.560
vegetarians consistently
had about 60% lower risk.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.840
And for bleeding strokes, about
65% lower risk than nonvegetarian.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.100
And this despite higher homocysteine
due to lower vitamin B12 intake,

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.500
which is what may have led
to the higher stroke risk

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:11.580
in the previous study.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.960
Overall, if you do a systematic
review of all the major studies,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.560
a comprehensive meta-analysis
found a significant protective effect

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.100
of a vegetarian diet versus
the incidence and/or mortality

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.280
from ischemic heart disease
and incidence from total cancer,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.540
with a vegan diet conferring about
twice the reduced risk cancer-wise.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.780
You can also look at it
the other way.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.860
What if you decide to stop eating
vegetarian and start eating meat?

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:41.160
The Adventist Health Study
looked at that and found that

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.080
compared to those
who stayed vegetarian,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.180
those who started
eating meat suffered

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.480
a 231% increased
risk of gaining weight,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.220
166% increase in the risk
of developing diabetes,

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.400
152% increased risk
of having a stroke,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.960
and 146% increased risk of being
diagnosed with heart disease.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:02.940
And if you keep eating meat,

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.220
you may cut your lifespan
by three and a half years.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.780
So better to not just cut out meat,
but cut it out for good.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.120
But it's not all-or-nothing.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.580
Even just cutting down may help.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.240
A food pattern that emphasizes
plant-derived foods was found

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.300
to be associated with a
reduced risk of all-cause mortality,

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.940
meaning living a
significantly longer life.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.020
So there are multiple benefits
even eating in the direction

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.140
of a more plant-based diet,
but what about any risks?

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.780
Despite concerns
for protein deficiency,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.480
adequate amounts of protein --
which means 0.8 grams

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.940
per kilogram of body weight,
which is about 50 grams a day --

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.140
can be consumed in a solely
plant-based diet, as seen among

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.440
the other billion plus people around
the world who don't eat meat.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.020
Vitamin B12 deficiency,
on the other hand,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:53.100
is a very real concern without
a regular reliable source,

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.200
and I have videos
on how to do that

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.200
either through supplements
or fortified foods.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.980
One benefit you don't hear much
about is the role our diets play

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.740
in the emergence of pandemic
infectious diseases,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.560
the subject of one
of my recent books.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.160
It doesn't take much for a virus
to jump from one animal to another,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.900
but there are no examples of plant
viruses ever jumping to humans

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.540
for the same reason
we don't ever come down

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.480
with a really bad case
of Dutch elm disease.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:25.260
The largest and oldest association
of nutrition professionals

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.000
in the world is clear:

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.080
plant-based diets are appropriate
for all stages of the life cycle,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.200
and may actually provide
health benefits for the prevention

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.420
and treatment
of certain diseases.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.140
For example, vegetarians and
vegans are at reduced risk

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.840
of ischemic heart disease,
type 2 diabetes,

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.960
high blood pressure, certain
types of cancers, and obesity.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.980
And to learn more, they encourage
people to go check out

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.900
a few good websites.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.220
As the emeritus dean of the School
of Public Health at Loma Linda

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.960
once said at a nutrition conference,

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.200
"Attitudes toward vegetarian
diets have progressed

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.220
from ridicule and skepticism,
to condescending tolerance,

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.040
to gradual and sometimes
grudging acceptance,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.926
and finally to acclaim."

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.420
In our next story,

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.060
we look at how healthier
plant-based diets compare

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.740
to unhealthy plant foods and
animal foods for diabetes risk.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:23.760
In my video on flexitarians,
I talk about how the benefits

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.580
of eating a plant-based diet
are not all-or-nothing.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.040
Simple advice to increase
the consumption

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.580
of plant-derived foods with
parallel reductions in the

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.340
consumption of foods from
animal sources was found to

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:40.080
confer a survival advantage,”
a live-longer advantage.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.340
They call it a pro-vegetarian eating
pattern, just moving in that direction,

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.580
as a more gradual, gentle
doable approach.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.060
If you’re dealing with a serious
disease, though, like diabetes,

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.300
avoiding some problem foods
completely may be easier

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.460
than attempting to moderate their intake.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.720
It’s like clinicians would never tell
alcoholics to simply cut down on alcohol.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.780
Avoiding alcohol entirely
is a more effective and,

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.200
ironically, easier for a problem drinker.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.220
Paradoxically, asking patients
to make a large change

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:15.180
may be more effective than
making a slow transition.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.120
Diet studies show that recommending
more significant changes

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.360
increases the changes that
patients actually accomplish.

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:25.980
It may help to replace the common
advice, ‘all things in moderation’

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.500
with ‘big changes beget big results.’

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.860
Success breeds success.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.040
After a few days or weeks of
major dietary changes,

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.100
patients are more likely
to see improvements

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.960
in weight and blood sugar levels—

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.140
improvements that
reinforce the dietary changes.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.920
Furthermore, they may enjoy other health
benefits of plant-based eating.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.200
that may give them further motivation.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.460
Those who choose to eat plant-based
for their health say it’s mostly

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.340
for general wellness and disease
prevention, or to improve

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.140
their energy levels or immune function.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:02.580
They felt it gave them a sense
of control over their health,

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.400
helps you feel better emotionally,
improves your overall health,

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:06.720
and makes you feel better.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.740
Most felt it was very important for
maintaining their health and well-being.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.160
For the minority that used it
for a specific health problem,

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.820
it was mostly for high cholesterol
or weight loss,

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.480
followed by high blood pressure
and diabetes, with most

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.760
reporting they felt
it helped a great deal.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.800
But others choose plant-based diets
for other reasons like animal welfare

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.400
or global warming, and it looks
like they’re more likely to be eating

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.580
things like vegan doughnuts,
sugary and fatty foods,

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.880
compared to those eating
plant-based because of

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.920
religious or health reasons.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:46.860
I mean the veganist vegan could bake
a cake using soda instead of eggs,

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.580
with frosting, covered in marshmallow
fluff and chocolate syrup,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:55.740
topped with Oreos, with a side of Doritos
dipped in, vegan bacon grease.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:01.920
But fruit for dessert… in the form of
Pop Tarts and Krispy Kreme pies.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:04.380
This, is a vegan meal.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.680
Yes, plant-based diets have
been recommended

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.600
to reduce the risk of type 2 diabetes.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:13.740
However, not all plant foods
are necessarily beneficial.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:18.360
Like in that pro-vegetarian scoring
system you got points for eating

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.840
potato chips and French fries,
just because they were technically

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.620
plant-based, but Harvard researchers
wanted to examine the association

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:31.260
of not only an overall plant-based diet,
but both healthy and unhealthy versions.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:34.200
So, they created the same kind
of pro-vegetarian scoring system

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.480
weighted towards any sort
of plant-based foods,

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:42.300
and against animal foods and then also
created a healthful plant-based diet index,

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.600
where at least some whole
plant foods took precedence

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.780
and Coca-Cola was no longer
considered a plant.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:53.280
Then lastly, they created an
unhealthful plant-based diet index

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.360
by assigning positive scores
to processed plant-based junk,

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.300
and negative scoring healthier
foods and animal foods.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:04.080
And then they found that a more
plant-based diet in general was good

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:08.280
for reducing diabetes risk, but eating
especially healthy plant-based foods

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.980
did better, nearly
cutting risk in half,

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:15.360
while those eating more
unhealthy plant foods did worse.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.860
Now, but is that because they
were also eating more animal foods?

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.100
People often eat burgers
with their fries;

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.460
so, they separated out the
effects of healthy plant foods,

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:29.280
less healthy plant foods,
and animal foods.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:32.820
And healthy plant foods were
protectively associated,

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.520
animal foods were
detrimentally associated,

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.705
and less healthy plant foods were more
neutral when it came to diabetes risk.

00:09:40.705 --> 00:09:43.080
Here’s what the graph looks like:
higher diabetes risk with more and more

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.980
animal foods, no protection
whatsoever with junky plant foods,

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.060
and lower and lower diabetes risk
associated with more and more

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.520
healthy whole plant foods in the diet.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:57.840
So, they conclude that yes, plant-
based diets are associated with

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.960
substantially lower risk of
developing type 2 diabetes,

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.740
but it may not be enough to just
lower the intake of animal foods,

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:09.830
but also less healthy
plant foods as well.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.080
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.780
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.600
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:22.140
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:28.200
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.780
go to the NutritionFacts
Podcast landing page.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:32.940
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:36.900
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:40.680
My last two books are
